VERDICT. Which arm is right at 13 declared bits changes with the
fold's arity, on committed harness output, with the stored column held
constant, at every tolerance tried. So the accumulator's width is not
recoverable from the stored value, and a design carrying it on the value
cannot fold one column two ways without a cast that changes no value.

Note which comparison answers this and which does not. The head-to-head
between the arity-derived accumulator and the fixed one has NO sign
change: the arity-derived arm wins at every arity. That is a finding
about those two arms and it does not answer the carrier question. What
answers it is the field: the best arm is the minimum container at arity
2 and the arity-derived accumulator from arity 4 upward, so the right
intermediate width for one stored column is two different things.

What this does NOT establish. It is one family, one declared width, one
element count, one transform, and one machine. It says the accumulator
question is answered by the fold in this region. Whether some other region
makes the stored value the right carrier is not touched.
